<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Simeon Uroš</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1326–1370</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Simeon Uroš Palaiologos, Siniša</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Simeon Uroš was a half-brother of the Serbian emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a> who governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> after Dušan's death and claimed the imperial title in 1356. Ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trikala">Trikala</a>, he relied on Albanian chiefs in Epirus, granting the title of despot to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-losha">Peter Losha</a> at Arta and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjin-bua-shpata">Gjin Bua Shpata</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angelokastron">Angelokastron</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simeon-uros.jpg" alt="Simeon Uroš" width="320" height="351" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: XIV century serbian painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Loza_Nemanjica_Decani_c_5_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
